1
00:00:26,113 --> 00:00:28,637
<i>NARRATOR:</i>
<i>Hear now the song of Troy,</i>

2
00:00:28,724 --> 00:00:32,032
<i>and of the years</i>
<i>the sea would not forget.</i>

3
00:00:32,119 --> 00:00:35,035
<i>Long ago, the kingdoms </i>
<i>of Greece united</i>

4
00:00:35,122 --> 00:00:36,732
<i>in a common cause...</i>

5
00:00:36,819 --> 00:00:38,734
<i>summoned by oath and honor.</i>

6
00:00:40,649 --> 00:00:44,653
<i>Their kings gathered</i>
<i>a mighty fleet</i>

7
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:47,873
<i>and sailed across the sea</i>
<i>to the great city of Troy.</i>

8
00:00:50,528 --> 00:00:53,488
<i>Its walls higher</i>
<i>than any siege tower,</i>

9
00:00:53,575 --> 00:00:56,665
<i> </i><i>thick enough that no battering</i><i> </i>
<i>ram could break them.</i>

10
00:00:56,752 --> 00:00:59,624
<i>A city that should</i>
<i>never have fallen.</i>

11
00:00:59,711 --> 00:01:04,934
<i>At its heart was Helen, wife</i>
<i>of Menelaus, king of Sparta,</i>

12
00:01:05,021 --> 00:01:08,285
<i>and when the Trojan prince</i>
<i>Paris carried her away,</i>

13
00:01:08,372 --> 00:01:12,376
<i>the kingdoms of Greece</i>
<i>arose as one.</i>

14
00:01:12,463 --> 00:01:18,078
<i>For ten long years,</i>
<i>they lay siege to Troy.</i>

15
00:01:18,165 --> 00:01:20,732
<i>Though countless warriors</i>
<i>fought and died,</i>

16
00:01:20,819 --> 00:01:23,692
<i> </i><i>the city remained unconquered.</i>

17
00:01:23,779 --> 00:01:27,261
<i>But where armies had failed,</i>
<i>cunning would prevail.</i>

18
00:01:27,348 --> 00:01:29,306
<i>Under the cover of darkness,</i>

19
00:01:29,393 --> 00:01:32,788
<i>Odysseus set a daring</i>
<i>plan into motion.</i>

20
00:01:32,875 --> 00:01:36,052
<i>Greek warriors entered</i>
<i>the city unseen.</i>

21
00:01:42,624 --> 00:01:46,497
<i>By dawn, Troy was</i>
<i>consumed by fire,</i>

22
00:01:46,584 --> 00:01:50,240
<i>and the long war</i>
<i>had come to an end.</i>

23
00:01:50,327 --> 00:01:53,809
<i>Yet for Odysseus,</i>
<i>patient as the tide,</i>

24
00:01:53,896 --> 00:01:56,072
<i>and cunning beyond all others,</i>

25
00:01:56,159 --> 00:01:59,293
<i>the longest journey</i>
<i>was just beginning.</i>

26
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:01,686
The King!

27
00:02:01,773 --> 00:02:03,384
King Odysseus!

28
00:02:03,471 --> 00:02:06,430
Our men are gone,
but Troy is fallen,

29
00:02:06,517 --> 00:02:07,997
and our battle is won.

30
00:02:08,084 --> 00:02:09,607
Where are we going?

31
00:02:09,694 --> 00:02:11,566
Home.
To my kingdom.

32
00:02:11,653 --> 00:02:14,003
To my son. My wife.

33
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:15,309
You may join me.

34
00:02:15,396 --> 00:02:17,137
Be an advisor
in my court.

35
00:02:17,224 --> 00:02:22,490
For your bravery in this
terrible war, I will ensure

36
00:02:22,577 --> 00:02:24,970
you will want for nothing
for the rest of your life.

37
00:02:25,057 --> 00:02:27,538
Thank you, my King.

38
00:02:27,625 --> 00:02:29,801
It would be my honor.

39
00:02:29,888 --> 00:02:32,413
The journey will not be easy.

40
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:36,025
I will swim if I must,
but I will get home.

41
00:02:39,463 --> 00:02:40,334
Trojans!

42
00:02:42,379 --> 00:02:43,511
If the gods are willing.

43
00:03:13,236 --> 00:03:16,892
Tell me, O Muse,
the man of many devices,

44
00:03:16,979 --> 00:03:20,112
who wandered full
many ways after he had

45
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:23,681
sacked the great
citadel of Troy.

46
00:03:23,768 --> 00:03:26,206
Home, then.

47
00:03:26,293 --> 00:03:27,946
Lest I have not honored the gods

48
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:30,558
enough to receive
the reward I deserve.

49
00:03:33,474 --> 00:03:36,216
Greetings, Odysseus.

50
00:03:36,303 --> 00:03:37,608
The good man.

51
00:03:37,695 --> 00:03:38,783
Bright-eyed Athena.

52
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:41,133
Goddess on high.

53
00:03:41,221 --> 00:03:42,657
Clever, maybe.

54
00:03:42,744 --> 00:03:44,311
Clever is better than good.

55
00:03:44,398 --> 00:03:45,355
Not just clever.

56
00:03:46,748 --> 00:03:50,752
Brutal, decisive,
and ingenious.

57
00:03:50,839 --> 00:03:54,103
Hiding your soldiers
in the gift of a wooden horse,

58
00:03:54,190 --> 00:03:55,800
your Trojan horse.

59
00:03:55,887 --> 00:03:58,890
Taking the city by surprise
from the inside out.

60
00:03:58,977 --> 00:04:00,196
Your greatest work.

61
00:04:00,283 --> 00:04:02,416
It did what it was made to do.

62
00:04:02,503 --> 00:04:04,287
But I cannot look behind.

63
00:04:04,374 --> 00:04:06,985
As any craftsman
worth his salt, I pray

64
00:04:07,072 --> 00:04:09,597
that my greatest
work lies ahead.

65
00:04:09,684 --> 00:04:11,773
Perhaps it does.

66
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:15,820
So home, then. Ithaca.
To your wife.

67
00:04:15,907 --> 00:04:17,213
Your city.

68
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:19,694
Yes, the only
journey worth the fight.

69
00:04:19,781 --> 00:04:21,609
Are you coming with me, Athena?

70
00:04:21,696 --> 00:04:24,002
Or is there another
reason for this visit?

71
00:04:24,089 --> 00:04:26,657
I come to bring you a warning.

72
00:04:26,744 --> 00:04:30,792
The gods,
they are fickle, Odysseus.

73
00:04:30,879 --> 00:04:34,665
They will not make your
journey home an easy one.

74
00:04:34,752 --> 00:04:38,234
Your trials will be
their entertainment.

75
00:04:38,321 --> 00:04:41,063
They know now that
you are a clever man.

76
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:43,152
They are jealous.

77
00:04:43,239 --> 00:04:46,634
They will use this against
you to show their power.

78
00:04:48,549 --> 00:04:51,029
Curse me or bless me,
Goddess on high.

79
00:04:51,116 --> 00:04:53,336
But be quick about it.

80
00:04:53,423 --> 00:04:55,599
In due time.

81
00:04:55,686 --> 00:04:58,123
And so begins your odyssey.

82
00:04:58,210 --> 00:05:00,517
And the game of the gods.

83
00:05:02,563 --> 00:05:04,434
Then pray for me, goddess.

84
00:05:04,521 --> 00:05:06,828
For I must reach home.

85
00:05:06,915 --> 00:05:08,612
For my kingdom.

86
00:05:08,699 --> 00:05:09,657
My everything.

87
00:05:09,744 --> 00:05:12,790
My son. My wife.

88
00:05:12,877 --> 00:05:14,052
My world will be gone.

89
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:31,766
I am looking for men.

90
00:05:31,853 --> 00:05:33,768
A crew for a dangerous journey.

91
00:05:35,726 --> 00:05:37,946
One the gods themselves
may turn their will against.

92
00:05:41,732 --> 00:05:45,170
Once I am home,
as king of Ithaca,

93
00:05:45,257 --> 00:05:49,914
I assure you each will
receive a suitable reward.

94
00:05:51,829 --> 00:05:55,006
You will never want for anything
for the rest of your lives.

95
00:05:56,704 --> 00:05:58,183
You have the word
of the king himself.

96
00:06:01,926 --> 00:06:04,451
A generous offer and reward.

97
00:06:04,538 --> 00:06:05,713
But are you as
good as your word?

98
00:06:07,715 --> 00:06:09,064
Polites!

99
00:06:09,151 --> 00:06:10,979
You look a little
bit past your prime.

100
00:06:11,066 --> 00:06:13,242
- By the gods!
- Old man.

101
00:06:13,329 --> 00:06:15,113
After all this time.

102
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:16,985
All this time and you still
haven’t gotten any better

103
00:06:17,072 --> 00:06:18,247
at motivational speeches.

104
00:06:19,770 --> 00:06:23,426
Absolute crickets
from you as well.

105
00:06:23,513 --> 00:06:25,950
This is a man who has saved
my life on numerous occasions.

106
00:06:26,037 --> 00:06:29,606
A debt we have both repaid
each other more than once

107
00:06:29,693 --> 00:06:32,566
in a war that has lasted
more than five years.

108
00:06:36,396 --> 00:06:37,701
Although it’s closer to ten now.

109
00:06:42,576 --> 00:06:44,142
But these days,

110
00:06:44,229 --> 00:06:47,885
I am much better collecting
scars than I am glory.

111
00:06:47,972 --> 00:06:50,888
But, you will find plenty
of much younger,

112
00:06:50,975 --> 00:06:53,630
probably not as bright men
more than willing to join you.

113
00:06:53,717 --> 00:06:55,458
But probably not here,

114
00:06:55,545 --> 00:06:57,547
sipping goblets in
the middle of the day.

115
00:06:57,634 --> 00:06:59,027
And the rest here,

116
00:06:59,114 --> 00:07:00,637
well, they know not
the true cost of war,

117
00:07:00,724 --> 00:07:03,161
though they may be eager.

118
00:07:03,248 --> 00:07:05,294
- As once we were.
- As we once were.

119
00:07:05,381 --> 00:07:08,645
Listening to our fathers
and our grandfathers stories.

120
00:07:08,732 --> 00:07:11,692
And just more than
willing to prove ourselves.

121
00:07:11,779 --> 00:07:13,433
Can I trust them?

122
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:14,912
About as much as
you can trust me.

123
00:07:16,871 --> 00:07:19,308
All I know is that what’s
waiting for you back in Ithaca,

124
00:07:19,395 --> 00:07:20,744
now that is your reward.

125
00:07:20,831 --> 00:07:22,267
And you deserve it, too.

126
00:07:22,354 --> 00:07:23,355
But for the rest of us...

127
00:07:26,707 --> 00:07:30,145
Join me.
I need men who I can trust.

128
00:07:30,232 --> 00:07:32,364
Men with
experience in battle.

129
00:07:32,452 --> 00:07:33,975
No, no, no. I will not be
going to any battle.

130
00:07:34,062 --> 00:07:35,803
Or any wars.
Ever again.

131
00:07:35,890 --> 00:07:37,979
I am unpersuadable.
I cannot be persuaded.

132
00:07:38,066 --> 00:07:40,851
Did I mention the gold?

133
00:07:40,938 --> 00:07:43,375
Well, did I mention
unpersuaded?

134
00:07:43,463 --> 00:07:44,420
When do we start?

135
00:07:44,507 --> 00:07:45,508
As soon as we recruit two more.

136
00:07:45,595 --> 00:07:46,814
- Two more?
- Yes.

137
00:07:46,901 --> 00:07:48,250
Okay. Let’s go.

138
00:07:50,208 --> 00:07:52,036
Alkimos the Navigator.

139
00:07:52,123 --> 00:07:55,387
From what I heard, he was
the one that saved a ship

140
00:07:55,475 --> 00:07:57,128
of fishermen from a cyclone.

141
00:07:57,215 --> 00:07:58,826
Really?

142
00:07:58,913 --> 00:08:01,785
I’ve heard of you,
King Odysseus.

143
00:08:01,872 --> 00:08:03,831
What have you heard?

144
00:08:03,918 --> 00:08:06,964
That you, sacker of cities,
son of Laertes,

145
00:08:07,051 --> 00:08:09,445
are favored by the gods.

146
00:08:09,532 --> 00:08:13,580
Not all the gods.
One or two. Maybe less.

147
00:08:13,667 --> 00:08:15,364
You survived the Trojan War.

148
00:08:15,451 --> 00:08:17,148
You’re a king.

149
00:08:17,235 --> 00:08:19,281
The King of Ithaca.

150
00:08:19,368 --> 00:08:21,283
I have a family
that comes from Ithaca.

151
00:08:21,370 --> 00:08:23,764
I haven’t seen them
since I was little.

152
00:08:23,851 --> 00:08:24,765
You know, my king--

153
00:08:24,852 --> 00:08:26,070
I’m not king today.

154
00:08:29,073 --> 00:08:30,466
But if you help me
get home, I will be.

155
00:08:35,253 --> 00:08:37,604
I’m a good navigator.

156
00:08:37,691 --> 00:08:39,388
But you mentioned gold.

157
00:08:39,475 --> 00:08:40,781
Did I?

158
00:08:40,868 --> 00:08:43,218
Oh. You did. Yes.
You mentioned gold.

159
00:08:43,305 --> 00:08:44,828
He mentioned gold.

160
00:08:44,915 --> 00:08:45,916
Hmm.

161
00:08:46,003 --> 00:08:48,005
I don’t seek gold.

162
00:08:48,092 --> 00:08:49,877
I’m bored.

163
00:08:49,964 --> 00:08:52,053
Bored of fishing
boats filled with old men

164
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:54,316
with putrid smelling cargo.

165
00:08:56,666 --> 00:08:57,798
I seek adventure.

166
00:08:57,885 --> 00:08:59,451
New shores.

167
00:08:59,539 --> 00:09:01,671
Like in the stories.

168
00:09:01,758 --> 00:09:02,803
Stories about you.

169
00:09:07,938 --> 00:09:09,679
Then join me,

170
00:09:09,766 --> 00:09:12,856
and we will make stories
that they will never forget.

171
00:09:12,943 --> 00:09:16,904
And I promise you, it will
be anything but boring.

172
00:09:16,991 --> 00:09:20,037
And once we reach Ithaca,
you can join my guard

173
00:09:20,124 --> 00:09:23,214
and help me protect
our fair kingdom.

174
00:09:23,301 --> 00:09:24,215
My king.

175
00:09:26,261 --> 00:09:28,176
Now, how are you at fighting?

176
00:09:28,263 --> 00:09:29,699
I can handle my own.

177
00:09:29,786 --> 00:09:31,614
But if you want a
force to be reckoned with,

178
00:09:31,701 --> 00:09:33,660
you want to
recruit Eurylochus,

179
00:09:33,747 --> 00:09:35,618
I’ve never seen another
man fight quite like he has.

180
00:09:38,621 --> 00:09:40,928
We’ll pay him a visit next.

181
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:46,629
So what say you, Eurylochus?

182
00:09:46,716 --> 00:09:49,632
I have killed a lot of
men to protect my home.

183
00:09:49,719 --> 00:09:51,939
War has plagued our
country for many years.

184
00:09:55,986 --> 00:09:57,858
How are you at sea?

185
00:09:57,945 --> 00:10:00,861
I’m a fast swimmer, and
even faster with a sword.

186
00:10:00,948 --> 00:10:02,471
Hm. Is that so?

187
00:10:02,558 --> 00:10:03,646
Hm.

188
00:10:05,169 --> 00:10:06,649
But now that
the war is over,

189
00:10:06,736 --> 00:10:09,086
I’ve found work at a
vineyard up the road.

190
00:10:09,173 --> 00:10:11,219
The owner, he treats me well.

191
00:10:11,306 --> 00:10:16,050
And his daughter,
Talia, she’s...

192
00:10:16,137 --> 00:10:20,010
I just don’t know if I can
commit to a journey this long.

193
00:10:20,097 --> 00:10:24,058
You will be in Ithaca
before sundown two days hence.

194
00:10:24,145 --> 00:10:26,060
We have to be.

195
00:10:26,147 --> 00:10:28,410
And if you decide to
stay in our fair kingdom,

196
00:10:28,497 --> 00:10:31,413
you will live a life
of ease in Ithaca.

197
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,850
And once there,
she can join you.

198
00:10:36,723 --> 00:10:38,507
If you teach me
how to be as fast

199
00:10:38,594 --> 00:10:40,596
as you, my king
Odysseus, then I’m in.

200
00:10:44,426 --> 00:10:46,515
And as I promised...

201
00:10:46,602 --> 00:10:48,082
We need gold.

202
00:10:48,169 --> 00:10:50,693
You said we need to
be to Ithaca in two days.

203
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:53,957
Yes. I have a plan.

204
00:10:54,044 --> 00:10:55,219
But you must trust me.

205
00:10:56,917 --> 00:10:58,222
I’m with you to
the end, my king.

206
00:11:00,355 --> 00:11:01,835
Or thereabouts.

207
00:11:01,922 --> 00:11:05,490
From Troy, Odysseus
<i>launched his journey home</i>

208
00:11:05,577 --> 00:11:08,885
<i>with 12 ships and 600 men.</i>

209
00:11:08,972 --> 00:11:13,194
<i>Back in Ithaca,</i>
<i>Odysseus’s dear wife Penelope</i>

210
00:11:13,281 --> 00:11:17,154
<i>dreams of her love</i>
<i>bearing the brutal seas</i>

211
00:11:17,241 --> 00:11:19,679
<i>to find his way home to her.</i>

212
00:11:46,488 --> 00:11:47,532
Odysseus?

213
00:12:06,726 --> 00:12:07,727
God’s above.

214
00:12:10,164 --> 00:12:12,340
You’ve shown me Odysseus lives.

215
00:12:14,472 --> 00:12:15,735
Now bring him home.

216
00:12:17,824 --> 00:12:20,740
Bring him home now,
or these lips

217
00:12:20,827 --> 00:12:23,743
shall never again
speak your names.

218
00:12:42,196 --> 00:12:44,198
A growing boy needs food.

219
00:12:44,285 --> 00:12:47,157
A boy needs many things,
mother, but I am not a boy.

220
00:12:47,244 --> 00:12:48,550
Not any...

221
00:12:50,987 --> 00:12:51,945
Mother.

222
00:12:52,032 --> 00:12:54,208
I’ve seen him, Telemachus.

223
00:12:54,295 --> 00:12:55,426
I’ve seen your father.

224
00:12:55,513 --> 00:12:57,037
You’re bleeding.
You must sit.

225
00:12:57,124 --> 00:12:59,735
No, my son.
We must stand and fight.

226
00:12:59,822 --> 00:13:01,171
What have you seen?

227
00:13:02,782 --> 00:13:03,695
A vision.

228
00:13:07,351 --> 00:13:08,265
A message from the gods.

229
00:13:11,094 --> 00:13:13,009
Troy burns.

230
00:13:13,096 --> 00:13:15,620
Your father, he is triumphant.

231
00:13:15,707 --> 00:13:18,493
He is coming home, but it
may not be fast enough.

232
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:21,583
Ithaca is vulnerable now.

233
00:13:21,670 --> 00:13:23,237
Now that the war is over,

234
00:13:23,324 --> 00:13:26,370
Odysseus must return to
reclaim the throne, otherwise

235
00:13:26,457 --> 00:13:29,765
another can claim this
kingdom as his own.

236
00:13:29,852 --> 00:13:33,813
Your father would rather
die than let that happen.

237
00:13:33,900 --> 00:13:35,553
He will return.
He must.

238
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:38,600
And in his absence,
I am the man of this house,

239
00:13:38,687 --> 00:13:40,950
and I say that we...

240
00:13:41,037 --> 00:13:43,518
You will be a man
soon enough, my love. You will.

241
00:13:43,605 --> 00:13:47,478
But you cannot be king.
Not yet.

242
00:13:48,915 --> 00:13:50,612
And therein lies our fight.

243
00:13:52,657 --> 00:13:56,400
Each day that your father
is away, others become

244
00:13:56,487 --> 00:13:59,664
more willing to claim
this kingdom as their own.

245
00:13:59,751 --> 00:14:04,887
If he does not return,
I will not also lose you.

246
00:14:04,974 --> 00:14:07,672
So we must be strong, my son.

247
00:14:07,759 --> 00:14:10,850
Together, we must
be like your father.

248
00:14:12,025 --> 00:14:14,244
Smart and resourceful.

249
00:14:14,331 --> 00:14:17,682
And protect this
kingdom without violence.

250
00:14:19,510 --> 00:14:20,424
Clever.

251
00:14:25,473 --> 00:14:28,302
So clever as to make
the gods jealous.

252
00:14:30,652 --> 00:14:33,655
Whether we want it or not,
the heavens will continue to

253
00:14:33,742 --> 00:14:36,527
move without Odysseus here
by our side where he belongs.

254
00:14:39,052 --> 00:14:40,967
If he does not return soon

255
00:14:41,054 --> 00:14:43,839
after victory
has been declared,

256
00:14:43,926 --> 00:14:46,146
then by law he will have
abandoned his duties as king.

257
00:14:48,322 --> 00:14:52,065
And a suitor, any suitor,

258
00:14:52,152 --> 00:14:54,894
may lay claim to this house,

259
00:14:54,981 --> 00:14:56,591
and to the crown of Ithaca.

260
00:14:56,678 --> 00:14:58,158
And to you.

261
00:15:00,290 --> 00:15:03,511
The law only reaches so far.

262
00:15:03,598 --> 00:15:08,342
When they come,
and come they will,

263
00:15:08,429 --> 00:15:10,257
we must be ready.

264
00:15:10,344 --> 00:15:15,523
And I am ready to fight for you
and for father and the kingdom.

265
00:15:15,610 --> 00:15:19,135
Son, this is not going
to be a sword fight.

266
00:15:19,222 --> 00:15:21,921
Or even a fight of fists.

267
00:15:22,008 --> 00:15:24,619
This will be a fight
of words and propriety.

268
00:15:27,100 --> 00:15:29,450
We need to buy him time.

269
00:15:29,537 --> 00:15:33,280
Keep the suitors busy.

270
00:15:33,367 --> 00:15:35,978
Their bellies full
and distracted.

271
00:15:37,632 --> 00:15:41,723
Now come and honor me
like you do him.

272
00:15:41,810 --> 00:15:44,813
The war is over now.

273
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:46,641
But our battle
is just beginning.

274
00:15:59,262 --> 00:16:00,829
Goddess Athena...

275
00:16:02,657 --> 00:16:04,528
Help us.

276
00:16:04,615 --> 00:16:06,269
I pray.

277
00:16:06,356 --> 00:16:08,880
But help Odysseus.

278
00:16:08,968 --> 00:16:10,099
Help my father.

279
00:16:11,971 --> 00:16:13,189
Please.

280
00:16:13,276 --> 00:16:15,931
The mighty storm
<i>that struck Odysseus</i>

281
00:16:16,018 --> 00:16:21,197
<i>ravaged the sea for ten days,</i>
<i>taking them far off course.</i>

282
00:16:21,284 --> 00:16:23,808
<i>After battling the sea,</i>

283
00:16:23,895 --> 00:16:27,029
<i>Odysseus has arrived</i>
<i>at his home in Ithaca...</i>

284
00:16:27,116 --> 00:16:29,162
<i>or so he thought.</i>

285
00:16:29,249 --> 00:16:30,554
This is not Ithaca, Athena!

286
00:16:33,166 --> 00:16:35,995
This is another one
of the gods’ cruel jokes.

287
00:16:39,824 --> 00:16:40,738
I must get home.

288
00:16:44,264 --> 00:16:45,613
I will get home.

289
00:16:52,576 --> 00:16:53,447
Penelope.

290
00:16:55,623 --> 00:16:56,667
Sweet Penelope.

291
00:16:59,105 --> 00:17:00,454
I will come home.

292
00:17:04,893 --> 00:17:06,895
Look at you keeping
step with the gods.

293
00:17:10,377 --> 00:17:11,595
Athena.

294
00:17:11,682 --> 00:17:13,945
I am many leagues off course.

295
00:17:14,033 --> 00:17:17,384
I’m only two days
journey from Ithaca.

296
00:17:17,471 --> 00:17:19,299
Please.

297
00:17:19,386 --> 00:17:22,737
Spirit me home to Ithaca,
great goddess.

298
00:17:22,824 --> 00:17:24,913
You know that I cannot.

299
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,090
It is not within
my power to do so.

300
00:17:28,177 --> 00:17:30,484
But think, Odysseus.

301
00:17:30,571 --> 00:17:33,965
Perhaps there is a reason
that you are not yet home.

302
00:17:34,053 --> 00:17:37,012
The gods have another plan.

303
00:17:37,099 --> 00:17:38,013
I see.

304
00:17:41,147 --> 00:17:43,497
I am here to help.

305
00:17:43,584 --> 00:17:46,978
I can offer you help three
times along your journey.

306
00:17:47,066 --> 00:17:49,198
Three gifts.

307
00:17:49,285 --> 00:17:50,678
Only three?

308
00:17:50,765 --> 00:17:52,810
’Tis all I am permitted.

309
00:17:52,897 --> 00:17:55,378
I am helping because
I see myself

310
00:17:55,465 --> 00:17:58,990
in your clever mind and
your tactician’s brain.

311
00:17:59,078 --> 00:18:02,559
Your glory in
the Trojan War honors me.

312
00:18:02,646 --> 00:18:05,519
And that is why I will help you.

313
00:18:05,606 --> 00:18:06,955
I am flattered, goddess.

314
00:18:08,739 --> 00:18:09,653
I must decline.

315
00:18:11,612 --> 00:18:13,353
I am offended.

316
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:17,357
Odysseus.
Refusing a gift from the gods?

317
00:18:17,444 --> 00:18:19,402
From Athena herself.

318
00:18:19,489 --> 00:18:22,840
If you cannot spirit
me home then please

319
00:18:22,927 --> 00:18:28,629
understand I must ask for
help only when I need it.

320
00:18:28,716 --> 00:18:33,329
When all hope is lost, my wits
abandon me and death beckons.

321
00:18:35,592 --> 00:18:38,813
In this moment I will
honor your generosity

322
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:41,294
for I know the
value of godly gifts.

323
00:18:43,426 --> 00:18:46,516
Then you must know even now

324
00:18:46,603 --> 00:18:49,650
dogs are sniffing
at your house

325
00:18:49,737 --> 00:18:51,260
and at your wife.

326
00:18:53,001 --> 00:18:56,265
The gods are testing
your cleverness, Odysseus.

327
00:18:59,312 --> 00:19:01,923
Then I pray the
gods do not stall me

328
00:19:02,010 --> 00:19:03,664
any longer than
they already have.

329
00:19:06,362 --> 00:19:08,408
Go, hero.

330
00:19:08,495 --> 00:19:10,497
Honor me and prove your worth.

331
00:19:11,672 --> 00:19:13,413
Get home.

332
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:16,720
No matter what horrors the
gods place upon your path.

333
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:27,078
The opportunity here
is too great to pass up.

334
00:19:27,166 --> 00:19:30,343
A kingdom, a crown,

335
00:19:30,430 --> 00:19:33,215
and even the queen herself

336
00:19:33,302 --> 00:19:35,870
all free for the taking.

337
00:19:35,957 --> 00:19:39,743
And it should be easy
enough owing to propriety

338
00:19:39,830 --> 00:19:42,311
and Grecian law.

339
00:19:42,398 --> 00:19:47,142
All suitors
must be taken seriously.

340
00:19:47,229 --> 00:19:48,361
And I will be the first.

341
00:19:48,970 --> 00:19:50,058
Ha!

342
00:19:51,451 --> 00:19:54,671
The early bird gets the kingdom.

343
00:19:57,065 --> 00:20:01,243
She’ll have no choice
but to honor my demands.

344
00:20:01,330 --> 00:20:02,636
All of my demands.

345
00:20:15,344 --> 00:20:18,869
Antinous just said
the most awful things.

346
00:20:18,956 --> 00:20:22,133
Threatening to take the crown,
our house, and you!

347
00:20:24,179 --> 00:20:25,615
Then it has begun.

348
00:20:25,702 --> 00:20:28,923
We must be on our
guard, Telemachus.

349
00:20:29,010 --> 00:20:31,404
Always polite and inviting.

350
00:20:33,754 --> 00:20:34,929
But on our guard.

351
00:20:38,976 --> 00:20:41,457
But on our guard!
